  "slug": "archaea-duplication-d927",
  "summary": "Sequence-based coding inventories now recover genes and peptides without relying solely on per-genome reference annotation.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread across these four items is building coding inventories from sequence evidence rather than depending solely on per-genome reference annotation. Genomes2Genes converts Progressive Cactus whole-genome alignments into validated coding sequence alignments for extant and ancestral species. A Chlamydomonas nivalis proteogenomic study mines sORFs from transcriptome data and a related-species genome to discover small encoded peptides without a reference genome. A comparative analysis maps verified protein complex components to orthogroups across 31 eukaryotes, identifying 184 universal orthogroups and releasing the PCOC suite. PANTHER 2027 extends protein family phylogenetic trees and GO annotations across 131 genomes. What is new is the use of cross-species genomic alignment, orthology, and family-level evidence to define coding content independently of a single reference annotation. The works differ in organism, data type, and objective, so this is a dominant thread rather than a shared method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-13"
}
